(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-oral-cavity-salivary-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Oral Cavity and Salivary Glands Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the gold standard for diagnosing epiglottitis?","choices":["The thumbprint sign on lateral neck X-ray","Computed tomography of the neck","Blood cultures","Mirror or fiberoptic laryngoscopy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Direct visualisation of the supraglottis is the gold standard, performed where the airway can be secured. The lateral neck X-ray thumbprint sign is recognised but explicitly not necessary for diagnosis, and computed tomography shows the same thing without adding safety. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is the commonest clinical manifestation of primary herpes simplex infection?","choices":["Herpangina","Hairy leukoplakia","Herpetic gingivostomatitis","A single cold sore at the lip margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary infection presents as herpetic gingivostomatitis, most often in seronegative children and young adults, with fever, malaise and cervical lymphadenopathy alongside the oral lesions. Secondary disease is reactivation of virus dormant in the trigeminal ganglion. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which gland is most commonly affected by acute suppurative sialadenitis?","choices":["The parotid","The submandibular","The sublingual","The minor salivary glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infection ascends the parotid duct and then spreads haematogenously, and salivary stasis is the initiating event. The gland becomes firm and diffusely tender with overlying erythema, trismus, purulent ductal discharge, induration, fever and chills, typically unilaterally in acute disease. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 36"},{"q":"What happens if a patient with mononucleosis is given penicillin?","choices":["The illness resolves more quickly","Anaphylaxis occurs","The spleen shrinks more rapidly","A widespread exanthem develops"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aminopenicillins reliably provoke a widespread maculopapular rash in Epstein-Barr infection. The classic sequence is a sore throat treated as streptococcal, a rash appearing, and then the correct diagnosis being made. Recognising it matters because it is not a true penicillin allergy. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 87"},{"q":"How are minor aphthous ulcers characterised?","choices":["Painless lesions healing within 24 hours","Under 1 centimetre, commonest, healing in 7 to 10 days","Over 1 centimetre with a risk of scarring","Numerous 1 to 3 millimetre ulcers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Minor aphthae are the commonest form, under a centimetre, preceded by burning and tingling, painful, and healing within 7 to 10 days without scarring. Major ulcers exceed a centimetre, are more painful, may scar and last over a month; herpetiform ulcers are numerous 1 to 3 millimetre lesions that also scar and persist. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 16"},{"q":"How is age scored in the Centor criteria?","choices":["Plus one for 3 to 14, zero for 15 to 44, minus one for 45 and over","Plus one for all ages","Minus one for children and plus one for adults","Age is not part of the Centor criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age is the only criterion that can subtract. Streptococcal pharyngitis is common in children and adolescents and uncommon over 45, so the score reflects that gradient, alongside one point each for absence of cough, tonsillar exudate, fever and tender anterior cervical nodes. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 76"},{"q":"How do unilateral and bilateral vocal cord paralysis differ in presentation?","choices":["Both present identically","Neither affects the voice","Unilateral gives a breathy hoarse voice; bilateral gives stridor","Unilateral gives stridor; bilateral gives a breathy voice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One immobile cord leaves a gap through which air escapes, producing a breathy hoarse voice with aspiration and dysphagia, and it may even be asymptomatic. Both cords immobile sit near the midline and obstruct the airway, giving inspiratory or biphasic stridor and a weak cry. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the characteristic pharyngeal finding in diphtheria?","choices":["A tenacious grey membrane over the tonsils and pharynx","Vesicles on the soft palate and tonsils","Creamy white patches that wipe away","Bilateral tonsillar exudate with palatal petechiae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pharyngeal form is commonest and produces an adherent grey membrane that bleeds when disturbed, with mild sore throat, fever, malaise, toxaemia and prostration. Complications come from the exotoxin: myocarditis with arrhythmia and heart block, and neuropathy affecting cranial nerves first. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 112"},{"q":"What are Fordyce granules?","choices":["Premalignant squamous lesions","Deposits of melanin","Fungal colonies within the epithelium","Ectopic sebaceous glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fordyce granules are normal variants: sebaceous glands appearing where sebaceous glands are not usually expected, on the vermilion of the lip and the buccal mucosa. They present as small yellow-white papules and need nothing but recognition and reassurance. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does laryngeal electromyography contribute in vocal cord paralysis?","choices":["It predicts whether function will return","It identifies which cord is paralysed","It measures airway calibre","It distinguishes vagal from recurrent laryngeal injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Electromyography distinguishes a nerve still in continuity from one that is not. A bruised or stretched nerve may recover over 6 months to a year, whereas a transected nerve or one infiltrated by malignancy will not, and that determines whether to wait or proceed to medialisation or thyroplasty. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the gold standard investigation for retropharyngeal abscess?","choices":["Lateral neck computed tomography","Lateral neck radiography","Ultrasound","Magnetic resonance imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography is the gold standard, showing a rim-enhancing hypodense collection in the retropharyngeal space and distinguishing abscess from adenitis, which is the decision that matters. A lateral neck X-ray showing a widened retropharyngeal space is supportive but less definitive. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 105"},{"q":"What is the classic triad of infectious mononucleosis?","choices":["Fever, rash and arthritis","Sore throat, cough and rhinitis","Fever, jaundice and abdominal pain","Fever, tonsillar pharyngitis and cervical lymphadenopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That triad defines the presentation, with palatal petechiae, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly and a maculopapular rash in about 5 per cent as additional features. About 90 to 95 per cent of adults are Epstein-Barr seropositive, and the illness commonly affects those aged 15 to 24. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which duct drains the parotid gland?","choices":["Stensen duct","Wharton duct","The sublingual ducts","The nasolacrimal duct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The parotid glands lie on the sides of the face and drain through Stensen duct, which crosses the masseter and opens opposite the upper second molar. That is where pus is expressed from in acute suppurative sialadenitis, which is characteristically a parotid disease. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why must contact sport be avoided for about a month after mononucleosis?","choices":["To prevent transmission to teammates","To avoid triggering the maculopapular rash","To prevent airway obstruction from tonsillar swelling","To prevent splenic rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epstein-Barr virus enlarges the spleen, and an enlarged spleen has a capsule under tension that can rupture with relatively minor abdominal trauma. The restriction runs until splenomegaly has resolved, monitored by ultrasound, because symptomatic recovery and splenic recovery do not follow the same timeline. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 86"},{"q":"What are Wickham striae?","choices":["Ectopic sebaceous glands on the lip","Lacy white lines on the buccal mucosa","Creamy patches that wipe off","Yellow-grey ulcer centres with a red halo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The reticular form of oral lichen planus produces lacy white lines called Wickham striae, most often on the buccal mucosa but also on palate, lips and tongue. They are part of the mucosa and cannot be wiped away, which distinguishes them from candidiasis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does a potassium hydroxide preparation show in oral candidiasis?","choices":["Multinucleated giant cells","Acid-fast bacilli","Gram-positive cocci in chains","Spores and pseudohyphae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Potassium hydroxide dissolves epithelial cells and leaves fungal elements visible, so spores and pseudohyphae confirm the clinical impression. Treatment is saline and peroxide washes plus a topical antifungal such as nystatin, clotrimazole, ketoconazole or fluconazole. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 90"},{"q":"Why should intraoral examination be avoided once epiglottitis is suspected?","choices":["It contaminates subsequent cultures","It may precipitate complete airway obstruction","It is uncomfortable for the child","It will not show the epiglottis anyway"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Once the diagnosis is suspected from history and general survey, further examinations that increase the child's anxiety, including intraoral examination and venipuncture, should not be performed, because they can complete the obstruction. Mirror or fiberoptic laryngoscopy in a controlled setting is the gold standard. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 63"},{"q":"What proportion of oral lichen planus progresses to squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["5 to 20 per cent","1 to 4 per cent, higher with ulcerative lesions","None; it is entirely benign","Around 90 per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The malignant potential is modest but real, and it is higher in ulcerative forms. That is the reason close follow-up is the point of making the diagnosis, alongside pain relief and removing reversible contributors such as medications, dental restorations, tobacco and alcohol. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for vocal cord nodules?","choices":["Speech therapy","Surgical excision of both nodules","Inhaled corticosteroid","Long-term antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nodules result from vocal abuse, so changing the vocal behaviour treats the cause rather than the lesion. Speech therapy is first line in both adults and children, with photodocumentation in the voice clinic tracking progress and microlaryngoscopy reserved for failure. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 47"},{"q":"Are antibiotics indicated in acute laryngitis?","choices":["Yes, in every case","Yes, but only intravenously","No, and antivirals should be given instead","No, unless a secondary bacterial infection is suspected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most acute laryngitis is viral, with rhinovirus commonest, followed by parainfluenza, respiratory syncytial virus, adenovirus, influenza and pertussis. Treatment is conservative, and antibiotics are explicitly not indicated unless bacterial superinfection is suspected. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which organism most commonly causes acute suppurative sialadenitis?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Candida albicans","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest pathogen, followed by Streptococcus viridans, Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pyogenes and gram-negatives, with Candida in chronically ill hospitalised patients. That is why treatment uses penicillinase-resistant gram-positive cover such as nafcillin or cefazolin. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 35"},{"q":"How do vocal cord polyps differ from nodules?","choices":["Polyps are unilateral and pedunculated","Polyps are bilateral and symmetric","Polyps occur only in children","Polyps are caused by human papillomavirus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nodules are bilateral, symmetric and paired; polyps are unilateral masses within the superficial lamina propria, pedunculated and sometimes with visible vascular markings. They are commoner in men with vocal abuse and heavy smoking, and are excised because a large one may conceal an occult early laryngeal carcinoma. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the commonest pathogen in paediatric epiglottitis?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Haemophilus influenzae type B","Streptococcus pyogenes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Haemophilus influenzae type B is the commonest pathogen in children, and routine immunisation has reduced the incidence of supraglottitis by over 90 per cent. Other organisms include Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus and beta-haemolytic streptococci, which is why the disease still occurs. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 60"},{"q":"Why do 80 to 90 per cent of salivary stones form in the submandibular gland?","choices":["It is the most exposed to oral bacteria","Its duct is long and uphill, and its saliva is alkaline and mucin-rich","It is the largest salivary gland","It produces the most saliva overall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Wharton duct runs a longer course and against gravity, and submandibular saliva has higher mucin and alkaline content with high concentrations of calcium and phosphate. Those conditions favour precipitation of calcium phosphate and carbonate, which is what a stone is. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 40"},{"q":"How long are contacts of a diphtheria case treated?","choices":["Penicillin for 30 days","Erythromycin for 7 days","No treatment is needed for contacts","Antitoxin for all contacts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contacts receive erythromycin 500 milligrams four times daily for seven days to eradicate carriage, while the case itself is treated for 14 days and isolated until three consecutive cultures after therapy are negative. Prevention rests on routine childhood immunisation with boosters. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 113"},{"q":"When can the monospot test be falsely negative?","choices":["Only if the patient has taken antibiotics","Never; it is always accurate","Early in the illness, in the first week","In patients over 40"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The heterophile antibody test is very sensitive and specific once antibodies have developed, so a positive result needs no further testing. But antibodies take time to appear, which is why a negative result in the first week does not exclude the diagnosis and serology for Epstein-Barr antibodies is used instead. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 84"},{"q":"What proportion of patients with systemic lupus have mucous membrane involvement?","choices":["About 75 per cent","About 40 per cent","About 5 per cent","About 90 per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Around 40 per cent of lupus patients have mucous membrane involvement, and oral lesions may be the FIRST sign of the disease. There is no correlation between the oral ulcers and systemic activity, so they cannot be used to judge control. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 23"},{"q":"What should be presumed if there is no improvement after 48 hours of conservative treatment?","choices":["The organism is fungal","Treatment should simply be continued for another week","An abscess has formed","The diagnosis was wrong and it is a tumour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Failure to improve within 48 hours of rehydration, antibiotics, warm compresses, massage and sialogogues means an abscess should be presumed. The gland becomes indurated with a dough-like consistency, and ultrasound or computed tomography locates the loculation for drainage or guided aspiration. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 38"},{"q":"What defines chronic laryngitis, and why does it matter?","choices":["Voice disturbance over 6 months, requiring surgery","Any hoarseness in a smoker, treated with antibiotics","Voice disturbance over 2 weeks, and it is not a true diagnosis","Voice disturbance over 3 days, treated with voice rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond two weeks the label is chronic laryngitis, but that is a description rather than a diagnosis: it obliges a search for the underlying cause. Laryngeal cancer and vocal cord polyps must be considered, which is why referral for laryngoscopy is the stated next step. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which symptom of peritonsillar abscess is most reliable?","choices":["Sore throat","Trismus","Uvular deviation","Fever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Trismus is singled out as the most reliable symptom. Pus tracking adjacent to the medial pterygoid irritates it into spasm, which limits mouth opening and also makes examination and drainage technically difficult. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 99"}]};
